  "query_preview": "..(After midnight Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 6:45 AM EDT THU SEP 3 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period on Friday evening, upper level \nridging is expected to extend from the Carolinas across the Southern \nAppalachians to the mid MS Valley and north to the Upper MS Valley as \nwell as southwest into west TX. Meanwhile, troughing associated with \nan upper low over the St Lawrence Valley/Quebec should extend into \nthe Northeast Conus to mid Atlantic states. This will all be in \nplace downstream of an upper low over the Pacific northwest and \nassociated trough over portions of the western Conus. Over the \nCommonwealth and OH Valley, northwest flow should be in place at the \nstart of the period as the upper trough extending into parts of the \neastern Conus digs farther south and ridging retrogrades/becomes \nestablished further westward in the Plains by late Friday night. \nChances for convection should linger into late Friday evening/night \nRain chances return to the area as early as Friday afternoon/evening \nas a shortwave approaches ahead of a weak cold front. Isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms will be possible with this feature, \nparticularly in the northern and eastern portions of the forecast \narea where height falls will be the most robust. Given the hot and \nmoist weather leading into Friday evening, any storms during the \nlate evening will have some instability to work with, a strong to \nperhaps severe thunderstorm are not off the table to begin the \nperiod. Atmospheric moisture will also be rather high, with LREF \nmean PW values in the 1.5-1.7 in range (80th percentile or higher \ncompared to climatology). As such, torrential downpours will also be \npossible within thunderstorms.\n\nBy late Friday night into Saturday morning, the aforementioned cold \nfront will sag south through much or all of eastern Kentucky, \nproviding another focus for showers and storms. There is still \nsignificant model disagreement regarding the timing and extent of \nconvective coverage associated with this frontal passage, resulting \nin NBM PoPs likely being a bit underdone at least at times compared \nto true rain chances, although uncertainty remains too high to \nrefine these PoPs at this time. Adding to this uncertainty, some \ndeterministic guidance depicts MCS activity or its remnants diving \nsoutheast out of the Midwest during the day on Saturday, following \nthe periphery of the ridge. Thus, although rain appears likely at \nsome point on Saturday, the details continue to remain unclear at \nthis time. For now, the NBM keeps PoPs generally low, maximized \nmainly in the 20-50% range, to account for this. Meanwhile, \nnortherly postfrontal winds will keep highs in the upper 80s on \nSaturday afternoon, with low 90s near the KY/TN line. \n\nRain chances look lower from Sunday onward as the upper level ridge \nbegins to expand to the east. With surface high pressure building to \nthe northeast of the forecast area, northeasterly winds will keep \nSundays temperatures cooler than at the start of the period (but \nstill quite warm). Dewpoints look to be modestly lower as well (in \nthe 60s rather than the 70s), and this drier pattern will continue \nthrough the end of the period. Even so, Labor Day looks to be quite \nwarm, with above average temperatures in the upper 80s expected \nacross the area. Other than this, the weather will be quite pleasant \nand sunny, with the exception of an isolated diurnally driven \nthunderstorm in south or southeast.",
